In article <567@ssp9.idca.tds.philips.nl> dolf@idca.tds.philips.nl (Dolf Grunbauer) writes:
>I have looked at the code and I agree with Paul. On the other hand I saw
>something in the code which  I don't like. In this particular case the
>following call is made (i.e. in mattacku() in src/mhitu.c):
>
>	rnd(10-ac); /*  where ac is the 10 - current AC */

I think you're misreading the code.  Go back and take a look at what happens
in mattacku().  Here's what I see happening:

     tmp = u.uac + 10;
     if (tmp < 10) tmp = 10 - rnd(10 - tmp);

>In the file src/rnd.c the heading of rnd() is:
>
>rnd(x)		/* 1 <= rnd(x) <= x */
>
>How can this postcondition be met when x < 1 ???
>So for very small AC (e.g. AC = -10) this function doesn't even work correctly!
>Maybe this explains why I always get hit so hard when having a very low AC :-(

If you'll look at the second of the two lines I listed, you'll notice that
there is an if statement which will always preclude the possibility of this
section passing an illegal (0 or negative) value to rnd.  Thus, the function
really does work, and perhaps the reason you always get hit so hard when you 
have a very low AC is just bad luck.  :-(

Basically what the code is doing (albeit in a convoluted way) is exactly
what it says it's doing:  If your AC is below zero, it will modify that to
an AC of a random number between your AC and -1.  Or, to be more true to
what the code does, if your AC+10 is below 10, it will modify your AC+10
to be a random number between your AC+10 and 9.  (Fun how convoluted the
nethack code can get at times, neh?)

